'''Tartessos''' is the first and innermost planet from the star [[Aurelius]]. It is a small, rocky world, approximately one-third the size of [[Adonia]], and resembles a battered, airless cinder suspended in the blinding glare of the inner system. Due to its proximity to the star, it possesses no significant atmosphere and experiences extreme surface temperatures. It is classified as an iron planet due to its unusually large metallic core relative to its crust volume.

The planet appears as a dark, charcoal-grey sphere heavily saturated with impact craters, giving it a texture similar to Adonia's moon [[Imera]] but significantly darker due to the chemical alteration of surface minerals by intense solar wind bombardment. Its surface is a mosaic of ancient basaltic rock and deep tectonic fractures caused by the immense tidal forces and heat of Aurelius. These fractures, known as rupes, indicate that the planet contracted as it cooled, while the ongoing tidal stress prevents the lithosphere from becoming completely static.

Tartessos orbits Aurelius at an average distance of 0.22 AU, completing a revolution every 38 standard days. Its orbit is highly eccentric, which, combined with the lack of an atmosphere to redistribute heat, results in the most extreme temperature variations in the solar system. The dayside surface temperature can exceed 700 K, sufficient to melt lead, while the nightside plunges to 100 K.

==Physical characteristics==
===Surface geology===
The surface of Tartessos is dominated by expansive plains of solidified [[w:basaltic lava|basaltic lava]], evidence of ancient volcanism that ceased billions of years ago. These plains are punctuated by impact craters of varying sizes, ranging from small bowl-shaped depressions to massive multi-ring basins. The regolith is composed of varying brownish and dark grey rock tones, darkening further near the equator where radiation weathering is most intense.

A defining feature of the Tartessian landscape is the global network of lobate scarps and deep tectonic chasms. These features are thrust faults formed as the planet's massive iron core cooled and contracted, causing the crust to buckle and crack. Additionally, the immense tidal forces exerted by Aurelius during perihelion passages induce periodic surface flexing, keeping these fracture systems geologically active and generating frequent moonquakes.

===Internal structure===
Tartessos has an extremely high density for its size, suggesting that it is composed of 70 percent metallic material and 30 percent silicate material. The core is believed to be enormous, occupying about 85 percent of the planet's radius. One theory for this anomaly is that Tartessos was once a larger planet that suffered a giant impact early in the system's history, which stripped away most of its original crust and mantle, leaving behind the iron-rich core.

==Name and etymology==
The name Tartessos is derived from a semi-mythical harbor city and civilization mentioned in ancient Insulonian history, famed for its wealth in metals, particularly silver and tin. The name was chosen to reflect the planet's metallic composition and its elusive nature against the glare of the sun. In [[Sanese language|Sanese]] astronomical nomenclature, the planet is known as Shakugansei (灼岩星), translating to the Burnt Rock Star, referencing its scorched and desolate appearance.

